    "case_id": "603",
    "case_summary": "An 84-year-old man with a medical history of hyperlipidemia, hypertension, hypothyroidism, and tobacco use was admitted for evaluation of an asymptomatic abdominal mass with pulsations. The patient had a 20-pack-year smoking history and had been smoking pipe tobacco for the past 10 years. The patient did not report shortness of breath, dizziness, nausea, vomiting, lower extremity weakness, or abdominal, back, or chest pain. A previous scan 9 years prior showed the mass was 3 cm.\n\nVital signs: Temperature - afebrile, Blood pressure - 120/76 mm Hg, Pulse rate - 73 beats/min, Respiratory rate - 16/minute. Examination revealed a well-defined large pulsatile mass palpable in the epigastric region with a soft bruit audible on auscultation. Pulses were palpable in the lower extremities bilaterally.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Complete blood count and electrolyte levels were within normal limits.\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  1. Computed tomographic angiogram (CTA) of abdomen and pelvis:\n    Image Title: Figure 1 - CTA Abdomen and Pelvis\n    Image Description: (A) Axial view showing a saccular aneurysm measuring 14.6 cm × 12.6 cm in greatest transaxial dimension. (B) Sagittal view showing 14.7 cm craniocaudal dimension. The right ureter was significantly displaced anterolaterally. Dense calcifications of iliac vessels present without narrowing. No signs of impending rupture. Aneurysmal neck angulation greater than 60 degrees with neck tortuosity noted.",
